# A village, found by its bell. `near(bell, 8)` is the marker every village has
# and the villager check is the second half: a villager within eight blocks
# means the body stands in one. Villages are rare. One may lie a few hundred
# blocks away, so the search is bounded and honest rather than exhaustive:
# `explore` walks for at most its 1200-tick budget per call, three failing
# calls fail this skill with not_found, and the caller decides whether to try
# again from a different place.
#
# Why the rules are ordered this way: both marker checks come first, so a body
# that already stands in a village ends without taking a step, and the cheaper
# bell check is above the villager check. `near_entity` answers unknown when no
# villager is in sight (it is never false), so the villager rule costs nothing
# on open ground and can never mask the search below it. The last rule explores
# for a bell: the bell is the block a village cannot miss, while
# `explore(villager)` is refused when the call starts, because villagers are
# not on the hunt allowlist. The explore's `not_found` is this skill's failure
# after its three tries.
skill find_village()
  doc "Walk until a village is near: a bell within 8 blocks or a villager within 8; fails not_found when the bounded search runs out."
  rules:
    near(bell, 8) -> done
    near_entity(villager, 8) -> done
    true -> explore(bell)
